From: Agent Browser contributors
Subject: [PATCH] Android product noise seeds: dedicated gates, payload parse, renderer forwarding (rev4)

rev4 extends rev3 with the missing renderer forwarding: the browser process
froze the payload but AppendExtraCommandLineSwitches only forwarded the
timezone switches to Android renderers, so the noise consumers (who run in
the renderer and parse the child process command line) never saw
agent-browser-noise-seeds. rev4 forwards agent-browser-product-profile and
agent-browser-noise-seeds alongside the timezone switches (the 0057 block).

rev3 fixes the r13o real-device finding: the noise payload was frozen under
agent-browser-noise-seeds, but RoxyFingerprintConfig only PARSES
agent-browser-fingerprint-config, which on Android carries the timezone
payload (no seed). seed_ stayed 0, enabled_ stayed false, and the canvas/
audio noise never applied on any device - different profiles shipped
identical fingerprints.

rev4 design:
- The noise-seeds payload is parsed for the noise fields only (schema
  version 1, canvas/audio dicts, decimal string seeds via exact uint64
  conversion). The historical fingerprint-config switch stays owned by the
  timezone config; JSON number seeds are rejected as a format because they
  lose precision above 2^53.
- enabled_ is not flipped on Android: the 35+ desktop consumers (UA,
  platform, fonts, plugins, webgl vendor spoof, ...) keep today's inert
  behavior there. The noise consumers instead gate on dedicated
  canvas_noise_active()/audio_noise_active() accessors.
- The noise switches are forwarded to Android renderers next to the 0057
  timezone switches; CopySwitchesFrom copies only switches that are
  present, so absent payloads change nothing.
- Desktop behavior is unchanged: on desktop the canvas gate equals
  enabled() && canvas_noise_enabled() (HashSeed never yields 0) and audio
  additionally requires positive amplitude, which only makes an existing
  arithmetic no-op explicit.

Runtime acceptance (cross-profile canvas divergence on a real device)
remains a separate requirement.

